REM ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
REM  genie.pni — the Prompt Operating System, as a cycler.
REM
REM  Every prompt here must CREATE, VERIFY, EXECUTE, MEASURE, FALSIFY or
REM  INTEGRATE an artifact. None of them describes one.
REM
REM  Each cycle must leave behind a more complete, executable artifact
REM  than the previous cycle.
REM ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

CYCLER GENIE
  TITLE   "Genie"
  OUTPUT  RESEARCH
  UNIT    "component"
  PURPOSE "Build a research artifact from nothing: discover, specify, formalize, implement, verify, simulate, experiment, falsify, document, audit, refine."

  CONTRACT
    REFUSE    "a step that produces prose ABOUT a thing rather than the thing"
    REFUSE    "a PASS with no evidence"
    REFUSE    "a measurement with no units and no uncertainty"
    REFUSE    "saying the research is complete"
    REFUSE    "an untagged statement, which would be read as derived"
    INVARIANT "a hypothesis never becomes a derived fact by restatement"
    INVARIANT "where it is not known, it says UNRESOLVED"
    EVIDENCE  "every component names what it still needs before it could be called done"
  END CONTRACT

  REM ── LAYER 0. The constitution of this piece of work. ──

  STAGE CONSTITUTION
    RESOLUTION EASY
    VERB   CREATE
    EXPECT JSON
    INTO   work
    ASK
      I am starting a piece of research. Set its constitution before anything is built.

      Reply with ONLY this JSON:

      {"question":"the question, as a question",
       "primitives":[{"term":"…","means":"…","is_not":"…"}],
       "evidence_would_be":"what would count as evidence here",
       "falsified_by":"what observation would kill this",
       "out_of_scope":["…"],"done_when":"…"}

      primitives: for each term I will rely on, say what it means AND what it must not be
      mistaken for. A forbidden substitution is worth more than a definition.
      falsified_by: if nothing could kill it, it is not a research question yet. Say so.

      What I have so far:
      {seed}
    END ASK
    VALIDATE HAS(work, "falsified_by")
    ON FAIL CLARIFY
  END STAGE

  REM ── LAYERS 1-2. What kind of object is this, and where does it connect? ──

  STAGE INTAKE
    RESOLUTION MID
    VERB   CREATE
    EXPECT JSON
    FROM   work
    INTO   work.object
    ASK
      Classify what I have brought.

      Reply with ONLY this JSON:

      {"type":"hypothesis|question|observation|equation|artifact|result|literature|implementation|correction",
       "claim":"…","depends_on":["…"],"unknowns":["…"],
       "status":"DEF|AX|ASSUMP|DER|CONJ|HYP|EMP|OPEN|FAIL"}

      status: one of the nine tags, and no other word. An untagged claim will be read as
      derived, which would be a lie.
      unknowns: what is genuinely not settled. An empty list here is almost always wrong.
    END ASK
    VALIDATE HAS(work.object, "status")
  END STAGE

  STAGE CORRELATE
    RESOLUTION MID
    VERB   VERIFY
    EXPECT TEXT
    FROM   work.object
    INTO   work.correlation
    ASK
      Where does this connect to work that already exists?

      In 80 to 150 words, separate four things and label each line:

        RETRIEVED  — read from a source that exists. Name the source.
        INFERRED   — concluded here, from something retrieved.
        PROPOSED   — offered now, resting on nothing yet.
        UNRESOLVED — known to be missing.

      Do not let one contaminate another. If you have no retrieved sources, say so under
      RETRIEVED rather than promoting an inference.
    END ASK
    VALIDATE MATCHES(work.correlation, "UNRESOLVED|RETRIEVED")
    ON FAIL REPAIR
  END STAGE

  REM ── LAYERS 3-4. Precise enough to be wrong. ──

  STAGE SPECIFY
    RESOLUTION EASY
    VERB   CREATE
    EXPECT TEXT
    FROM   work
    INTO   work.spec
    ASK
      Write the specification. Precise enough to be WRONG.

      Give: definitions, assumptions and their justification, variables with their domains,
      constraints, the mapping being claimed, predictions, failure conditions, validation
      criteria, and the limits of scope.

      Tag every statement with one of DEF AX ASSUMP DER CONJ HYP EMP OPEN FAIL.
      Where a quantity is not known, write UNRESOLVED. Do not supply a plausible value.
    END ASK
    VALIDATE WORDS(work.spec) > 80
    ON FAIL REPAIR
  END STAGE

  STAGE FORMALIZE
    RESOLUTION MID
    VERB   CREATE
    EXPECT TEXT
    FROM   work.spec
    INTO   work.formal
    ASK
      Convert the specification into formal statements: definitions, axioms, lemmas,
      propositions, theorems, conjectures, derivations, counterexamples.

      Every line carries its tag. A conjecture may not become a theorem by restatement, and
      a derivation that does not exist yet is marked OPEN, not DER.

      Name the formalization targets — what a proof assistant would have to be given.
    END ASK
  END STAGE

  REM ── LAYER 5. Build the thing. Do not merely explain it. ──

  STAGE IMPLEMENT
    RESOLUTION EASY
    VERB   CREATE
    EXPECT DATA
    FROM   work.formal
    INTO   work.impl
    ASK
      Build it. Not a description of it — the thing.

      Produce whichever applies: a formal specification file, a proof-assistant skeleton, a
      simulator, an implementation, a measurement pipeline, a test suite, a container
      definition, a benchmark.

      Return the artifact itself. Say explicitly which components are still missing, and
      name each one.
    END ASK
    VALIDATE NOT EMPTY(work.impl)
    ON FAIL REPAIR
  END STAGE

  STAGE VERIFY
    RESOLUTION MID
    VERB   VERIFY
    EXPECT TEXT
    FROM   work.impl
    INTO   work.verification
    ASK
      Independently attack what was just built. You are not confirming it.

      Check: type correctness, dimensional consistency, mathematical consistency, logical
      consistency, conservation, boundary conditions, limiting cases, symmetries, numerical
      stability, reproducibility, implementation equivalence, contradictions with established
      results, counterexamples, hidden assumptions.

      Report each as PASS, FAIL, BLOCKED or PARTIAL. A PASS with no evidence is an assertion,
      not a verification — give the evidence or write BLOCKED.
    END ASK
    VALIDATE MATCHES(work.verification, "PASS|FAIL|BLOCKED|PARTIAL")
    ON FAIL REPAIR
  END STAGE

  STAGE SIMULATE
    RESOLUTION MID
    VERB   EXECUTE
    EXPECT DATA
    OPTIONAL
    ATTACH INBOX
    FROM   work.impl
    ASK
      Run it and bring back what happened — not what should happen.

      Return the run: parameters, initial conditions, outputs, and how somebody else would
      reproduce it. If it did not run, say that instead, and say where it stopped.
    END ASK
  END STAGE

  REM ── LAYER 8. From a computational claim to a physical one. ──

  STAGE EXPERIMENT
    RESOLUTION MID
    VERB   MEASURE
    EXPECT TEXT
    FROM   work.spec
    INTO   work.experiment
    ASK
      Translate the claim into something measurable in the world.

      Give: the measurable quantity, the instrument, the protocol, the control, the expected
      result, and the discriminating observable — the one that separates this claim from the
      nearest rival.

      Every number carries its units and its uncertainty. A number without them is not a
      measurement.
    END ASK
    VALIDATE NOT EMPTY(work.experiment)
  END STAGE

  REM ── LAYER 9. Try to kill it. ──

  STAGE FALSIFY
    RESOLUTION EASY
    VERB   FALSIFY
    EXPECT TEXT
    FROM   work
    INTO   work.falsification
    ASK
      Assume this is wrong. Find the smallest thing that would prove it.

      Give the hypothesis, the prediction, the null model, the control, the discriminating
      observable, the falsification threshold, and the result if it has been run.

      You are trying to kill it. If you find nothing, say exactly what you tried — a
      falsification step that reports "no problems found" without saying what it attempted
      has done nothing.
    END ASK
    VALIDATE WORDS(work.falsification) > 50
    ON FAIL REPAIR
  END STAGE

  REM ── LAYER 10. What is now true, and what is not. ──

  STAGE SYNTHESIZE
    RESOLUTION EASY
    VERB   INTEGRATE
    EXPECT TEXT
    FROM   work.verification
    INTO   work.synthesis
    ASK
      Update the position, and only where a lower-level artifact now exists to support it.

      Say separately: what is now PROVEN · what is COMPUTATIONALLY VERIFIED · what is
      EMPIRICALLY SUPPORTED · what remains HYPOTHETICAL · what FAILED · what CHANGED · what
      DEPENDS ON WHAT · what should be done next.

      Do not promote anything a tier it has not earned.
    END ASK
  END STAGE

  STAGE AUDIT
    RESOLUTION EASY
    VERB   VERIFY
    EXPECT JSON
    FROM   work
    INTO   work.completeness
    ASK
      Measure progress, not ego.

      Reply with ONLY this JSON:

      {"components":[{"name":"…","required":true,"state":"done|partial|missing","evidence":"…"}],
       "missing_named":["…"],"statement":"…"}

      statement: it may not say the research is complete. Where every required component
      exists, the honest sentence is that they exist — which is not the same as correct.
      evidence: for anything marked done, what exists that shows it.
    END ASK
    VALIDATE HAS(work.completeness, "components")
    ON FAIL REPAIR
  END STAGE

  STAGE REFINE
    RESOLUTION MID
    VERB   INTEGRATE
    EXPECT TEXT
    FROM   work.completeness
    INTO   work.next
    ASK
      Write the starting artifact for the next cycle.

      Name the single component whose absence blocks the most, say what producing it would
      take, and say what would make it unnecessary.

      Then state the one thing that, if it were false, would waste all of the above.
    END ASK
  END STAGE

  STAGE SEAL
    RESOLUTION PRO
    VERB  INTEGRATE
    GATE  HUMAN "SEAL genie"
    EMIT  MANIFEST "MANIFEST.sha256"
    ENVELOPE "sealing fixes what this cycle produced; it does not assert that it is right"
  END STAGE
END CYCLER
